WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
We are about to embark on a journey that will turn you into a capable responder for your community, your workplace, and your family.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Before we dive into the how-to, I want to ask, has anyone here ever had to call 911?

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.000
If you have, you probably remember that heart-pounding rush of adrenaline and the feeling of uncertainty.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:29.000
We are here today to give you the structure and the skills to manage those nerves and take effective action.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
On the screen, you see our roadmap for the course.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.000
We'll be moving through these steps one at a time, building your skills from the ground up.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Recognizing the emergency and first actions, we start with the most important rule, safety first.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:53.000
You cannot help anyone if you become a victim yourself, so we'll learn how to assess a scene before you ever touch a patient.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Assessment and activation, you'll learn how to check for responsiveness and breathing and how to get professional help on the way fast.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Adult and child CPR, this is our core hands-on block.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:13.000
You'll get plenty of practice with the mannequins you see here, mastering the right depth and rhythm for compressions.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Using an AED, don't let the technology intimidate you.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:24.000
You'll learn exactly how to use an AED trainer and see why these devices are designed for anyone to operate.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Putting it all together, you'll each participate in a scenario to practice and reinforce the skills you just learned in a real time.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:38.000
We'll practice these skills individually and learn how to get help from others and work as a team.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Infant CPR, we will switch to infant mannequins to learn the specific, gentle techniques needed for the smallest patients.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Choking, we save this for last because it is one of the most common real-world emergencies you might actually encounter.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Before we start, at the top of the list, just a quick housekeeping note.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.000
Please make sure you've each signed in on the class roster and feel free to ask questions as we go.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Ready to get started? Let's begin with the first step on our roadmap, recognizing the emergency.

